#!/usr/bin/env python3
# program-init -- the DETERMINISTIC CARRIER for the owner's program card.
#
# THE WALL THIS REMOVES (measured on a fresh mint, 2026-09-13 and again
# 2026-09-14). The owner says "운동 시작" on a finished install and gets:
#
#     ERROR: program.yaml not found: .../files/program/program.yaml
#     session-ensure: session-open exit=1 (task gate continues)
#     CREATED	1	헬스
#
# and one step past it, with a program card in place:
#
#     ERROR: unknown session_type code '가A' (valid: )
#
# Both halves were the SAME hole. `files/program/program.yaml` had no template
# and no creator anywhere in the tree -- the only copy shipped was
# tests/fixtures/program.yaml, whose own header says "Not anyone's training."
# `session_type` had no production writer: only test fixtures inserted rows, so
# the birth tag failed and every card afterwards carried
#     SESSION_UNTAGGED ... the split shown was INFERRED, not read
# The rotation codes ARE program.yaml's `rotation:` list. A design that produces
# the file but leaves the table to some other hand has not removed the wall, it
# has moved it one step right. So ONE path produces both, and the DB write is a
# side effect of the file write, not a second errand.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT THIS IS NOT: a default program (D1 -- the user owns Why)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# There is no shipped 5x5 here and there never will be. Every training decision
# in the output -- which days exist, what they are called, which lifts, how many
# sets, what rep range, what weight -- comes from the owner's answers and from
# nowhere else. This file owns the PATH those answers travel and the GUARANTEE
# that the path completes; it does not own their content. The one thing it adds
# on its own is equipment MECHANICS already declared by the pack's own shipped
# taxonomy (which lattice a barbell is on), and every such derivation is
# announced in the written file and overridable by an explicit answer.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY A CLI AND NOT A SENTENCE IN A SKILL (D2 -- mechanism > discretion)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# workout-program/SKILL.md already said "Generate files/program/program.yaml per
# the program.yaml spec below" and had said it for as long as the pack existed.
# On a fresh mint the file was still absent. A model that is ASKED to write a
# file at the end of an interview sometimes does not, and nothing notices --
# which is the failure mode of every one of the nine gaps the 2026-09-12 audit
# found. The repair is the D2 enforcement ladder, rung 1: tool-enforced
# semantics.
#
#   * the model never writes program.yaml. It ANSWERS; this file writes.
#   * the write is not a step the interview can skip: it FIRES AS THE SIDE
#     EFFECT of the last required answer landing (`answer` -> WROTE + SYNCED).
#     There is no "and then write it" instruction left to forget.
#   * a partial interview produces NO FILE AT ALL. Never a half-program that
#     session-open would happily open and prescribe from.
#   * what is still missing is a QUERY, not a memory: `next` says what to ask,
#     `check` says whether the install is finished, and both are read by the
#     install lane and by session-open's banner.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE LANE SEAM, NAMED (D10 -- assign each step a lane at design time)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   LLM lane   -- asking the owner, in his language, one question at a time;
#                 hearing "펜들레이 로우 60키로 3세트" and turning it into
#                 {name, display, weight_kg, sets}; judging the G19 RIR roster
#                 (whose single home is workout-program/SKILL.md, not here).
#   RULE lane  -- the SCHEMA those answers must satisfy (`schema`); WHICH
#                 question is outstanding (`next`, a state machine over a draft
#                 file, not the model's recollection); validation (`answer`
#                 refuses and names the field); the equipment derivations;
#                 the WRITE; the session_type derivation; the verdict (`check`).
#   The seam   -- `answer`. Everything upstream of it is judgment, everything
#                 downstream of it is arithmetic, and it is a typed boundary
#                 that either accepts a well-formed answer or refuses it by
#                 name. Per D10's test: "same input -> same required output?"
#                 is NO for the interview (that is why it stays LLM) and YES for
#                 everything after the answer document exists (that is why none
#                 of it is left in prose).
#   Verify rung-- one derivation is judgment the rule lane must NOT make: the
#                 G19 RIR floor. So the rule lane does not compute it; it
#                 CHECKS COVERAGE and names every slot in the G19 target class
#                 that carries no floor (W-G19-UNSET). Rule outputs stay
#                 value|gate|verify; a claim about the owner's training stays
#                 in the LLM lane with provenance.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE WRITE IS ROUND-TRIPPED, NOT ASSUMED
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# program.yaml is read by a HAND-ROLLED parser (rx-calc.load_yaml) -- not PyYAML
# -- so "we emitted a dict" is not evidence the engine will read that dict back.
# Every write goes: render -> write to a temp file -> load it back THROUGH
# rx-calc's own loader -> compare the reloaded structure against the intended
# one -> only then os.replace() into place. A mismatch is E-ROUNDTRIP and the
# live file is never touched. This is what makes "fails loudly at write time
# rather than at session-open time" true rather than hoped.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Usage
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   program-init schema  [--json]        the answer contract, machine-readable
#   program-init status  [--json]        where this instance is
#   program-init next    [--json]        the next outstanding stage, or done
#   program-init answer  --stage <s> --json <inline|@file|->
#                                        validate + record; the LAST required
#                                        stage also WRITES and SYNCS
#   program-init write   --answers <@file|-|inline>
#                                        one-shot: a whole answer document
#   program-init sync    [--dry-run]     derive routine + session_type rows
#                                        + the us_step rows serve resolves
#   program-init validate [--program P]  validate an existing card
#   program-init check                   verdict for the install lane
#   program-init reset                   discard the draft (never the card)
#
#   common: --db PATH --program PATH --draft PATH --force
#
# Exit codes (shared by every subcommand):
#   0  ok / complete
#   1  usage or environment error (unreadable DB, bad argument)
#   2  REFUSED -- the answer or the card is invalid; every error is named
#   3  UNMET -- nothing is wrong, the work has not been done yet (`check`,
#      `next` with an outstanding stage). Mirrors health_taxonomy_seed.py
#      --check, which the install lane already speaks.
#   4  the card was written but the DB derivation failed. The card is real and
#      valid; session_type is not. Reported, never swallowed.
#
# stdout is machine-parseable TAB lines; prose goes to stderr.
# stdlib only. ASCII source except where a user-visible Korean literal is
# unavoidable (the makeup code, which comes from the kit's own vocabulary).

