# Equipment class -> the lattice the pack can honestly derive.
#
# THE DIVIDING LINE IS "is this a fact about the CLASS of equipment, or about
# ONE GYM'S ONE MACHINE". The first is shipped taxonomy and is derived here; the
# second is measured at the machine by the F17 JIT lane that already exists
# (rx-calc.needs_lattice -> workout-session/reference/lattice-jit.md ->
# service/health/lattice-set), and this tool does not pre-empt it with a guess.
#
#   barbell            an Olympic bar is 20kg everywhere and plates come in
#                      2.5kg pairs -> the full standard grid, floor = the empty
#                      bar. This is also the one class the PLATE CUE runs on.
#   ez_bar / t_bar     the 2.5kg plate grid is the same, but the BAR's own
#                      weight is not universal (EZ bars run 7-12kg) -> type
#                      only, no base. snap_to_lattice then floors at 0.0, which
#                      under-constrains rather than lying about the floor.
#   dumbbell           fixed bells are even-kg -> `even`, whose own defaults
#                      (base 0.0 / step 2.0) are exactly the class fact. This
#                      entry is also what keeps the #39-B pair fixed: `even` is
#                      a NON_PLATE_LATTICE_TYPE, so a dumbbell press never gets
#                      a "바 20kg + 양쪽 4kg" cue even though ax_exercise
#                      correctly calls it compound+freeweight.
#   bodyweight_assist  an assist stack: type known, grid measured at the machine.
#   machine / cable /  base+step ARE the specific machine. snap_to_lattice
#   smith / v_squat    refuses to guess a malformed stack ("do not guess") and
#                      needs_lattice raises the JIT ask once the owner marks
#                      `loading`. Emitting a made-up base here would silence
#                      both.
LATTICE_BY_EQUIPMENT = {
    "barbell":           {"type": "standard", "base": 20.0, "step": 2.5},
    "ez_bar":            {"type": "standard", "step": 2.5},
